{"fileId":"1ICiJOBXf4bOrbtI69Akv1e0yvX_KdCEG","sourceSha256":"43e7f44be32ad21f7f957faadbf2cc6f6d40a56b1c1193bd53074cbcde652a83","blocks":{"9efcfbfa8b2c743b9002":{"type":"heading","page":5,"text":"ZERO CONDITIONAL SENTENCES or PRESENT REAL CONDITIONAL","bbox":[72.0250015258789,116.66802978515625,463.3199768066406,129.4830322265625]},"23c78202d57473e0c69c":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"In a Conditional sentence, we follow TWO PARTS of the sentence,","bbox":[108.05000305175781,131.468017578125,466.28997802734375,144.30502319335938]},"9e03646fcf7c473307d1":{"type":"table","rows":[["IF CLAUSE","+","MAIN CLAUSE"],["CONDITION","","RESULT"],["OR","",""],["MAIN CLAUSE","+","IF CLAUSE"],["RESULT","","CONDITION"]],"bbox":[147.41667048136392,159.1775131225586,455.6133346557617,276.9300308227539],"text":"IF CLAUSE + MAIN CLAUSE CONDITION  RESULT OR   MAIN CLAUSE + IF CLAUSE RESULT  CONDITION","page":5},"b355d60b191cd33788d8":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"*note: the If Clause is also known as the Dependent Clause and the Main Clause is the Independent Clause.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,294.9760437011719,505.6327209472656,325.16998291015625]},"6a94a674baa243cbb4a8":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"What are the USES of ZERO CONDITIONAL SENTENCES?","bbox":[108.05000305175781,327.36602783203125,461.41998291015625,341.3700256347656]},"85e0e1ba6bcc3b5d4aa4":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"✓is used to make statements about the real world ✓refers to general truths, like scientific facts ✓the time is now or always","bbox":[144.0500030517578,343.5660095214844,440.989990234375,389.9700012207031]},"0ecbe122eaab21979c58":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"How do we FORM ZERO CONDITIONAL SENTENCES?","bbox":[72.0250015258789,408.3960266113281,405.6299743652344,422.4000244140625]},"4aba4eaa0f0f7f028adc":{"type":"table","rows":[["IF CLAUSE (CONDITION)","MAIN CLAUSE or RESULT"],["IF + simple present tense of the VERB","Simple present tense of the VERB"],["or",""],["MAIN CLAUSE or RESULT","IF CLAUSE (CONDITION)"],["Simple present tense of the VERB","IF + simple present tense of the VERB"]],"bbox":[82.39166577657063,432.23333740234375,520.8133646647135,548.1966145833334],"text":"IF CLAUSE (CONDITION) MAIN CLAUSE or RESULT IF + simple present tense of the VERB Simple present tense of the VERB or  MAIN CLAUSE or RESULT IF CLAUSE (CONDITION) Simple present tense of the VERB IF + simple present tense of the VERB","page":5},"c24fb97f86aaac45ae47":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"Examples:","bbox":[144.0500030517578,548.6160278320312,208.9199981689453,562.6200561523438]},"1cde2969569644c936ef":{"type":"heading","page":5,"text":"If you heat ice,","bbox":[157.4499969482422,581.4159545898438,255.97999572753906,595.39599609375]},"6a9e2fcba21e47f0d8d8":{"type":"heading","page":5,"text":"It melts.","bbox":[382.1300048828125,581.4159545898438,439.42999267578125,595.39599609375]},"2afd7a3cb7476b7b2ce4":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"IF CLAUSE (Condition)","bbox":[137.64999389648438,597.615966796875,275.739990234375,611.6199951171875]},"b21309d341188d3c8e8e":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"MAIN CLAUSE (result) Verb in the Simple Present","bbox":[343.1300048828125,597.615966796875,490.9579162597656,627.8500366210938]},"27b56cedfedeb25235b3":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"Verb in the Simple Present Tense","bbox":[107.25,622.0459594726562,305.94000244140625,636.0499877929688]},"210c3369d904e4cda411":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"Tense","bbox":[391.75,630.0459594726562,429.5899963378906,644.0499877929688]},"e6ebc1aa2b3e9ffc69a1":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"or we can also do this…","bbox":[231.27000427246094,646.2459716796875,375.16998291015625,660.25]},"d781363d2af1ecb1d380":{"type":"heading","page":5,"text":"Ice melts","bbox":[175.8800048828125,662.4459838867188,237.55999755859375,676.426025390625]},"a1407e4a73aafd191391":{"type":"heading","page":5,"text":"if you heat it.","bbox":[366.1300048828125,662.4459838867188,455.6299743652344,676.426025390625]},"6ce0a577a989f1c0dea9":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"MAIN CLAUSE (result","bbox":[140.85000610351562,678.64599609375,272.3399963378906,692.6500244140625]},"e1b2c406b9227af1044e":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"IF CLAUSE (Condition Verb in the Simple Present","bbox":[343.5199890136719,678.64599609375,490.9257507324219,708.8500366210938]},"3e4c7ab5358f7fc0658d":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"Verb in the Simple Present Tense","bbox":[107.25,703.0459594726562,305.94000244140625,717.0499877929688]},"be411234e79750154c07":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"Tense","bbox":[391.75,711.0459594726562,429.5899963378906,725.0499877929688]},"62219de2ea740a01f14a":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"*discussion source:https://www.ef.com/wwen/english-resources/english-grammar/zero-conditional/","bbox":[72.0250015258789,741.9550170898438,518.56005859375,753.625]},"fbe084b46c1e5ca7ed14":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"Quick Review: (Subject-Verb Agreement)","bbox":[72.0250015258789,86.91596984863281,312.5400085449219,100.91996765136719]},"bcdac6a925d3bcce2a48":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"Understanding conditional sentences might be a bit confusing to you, but this lesson will help you ease the discomfort of learning it. Together, let us learn the easy way, this module will help you to understand it better.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,273.3760070800781,535.0400390625,319.780029296875]},"796a551743c2d9377d6d":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"We often use these sentences in our daily conversations; we have to be","bbox":[108.05000305175781,321.96600341796875,535.0400390625,335.9700012207031]},"332f72e9b45a43960eb8":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"aware of its importance and the proper way of using it in sentences. This way, we can build confidence not just in writing but also in speaking.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,338.176025390625,535.0400390625,368.3700256347656]},"094b8511819977e48212":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"In this lesson, discover the fun and easy way of learning conditional sen-","bbox":[108.05000305175781,370.5660095214844,531.1799926757812,384.57000732421875]},"d66b889549a4f31ff8bf":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"tences.","bbox":[72.0250015258789,386.7960205078125,117.09000396728516,400.8000183105469]},"3e1f0b0ed2d79e99e10f":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"CLAUSES is a group of words that consist of a subject and a predicate; it may and may not stand alone. ✓ INDEPENDENT CLAUSE (also known as MAIN CLAUSE)","bbox":[72.0250015258789,419.1960144042969,533.4535522460938,465.57598876953125]},"bc271189183d0bb23968":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"a group of words that has a subject and a predicate and can stand alone, it can act as a complete sentence because it has a complete thought","bbox":[144.0500030517578,467.7960205078125,516.9014892578125,514.22998046875]},"fccac3bdf8ec39ebb881":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"✓ DEPENDENT CLAUSE (also known as SUBORDINATE CLAUSE)","bbox":[72.0250015258789,516.4259643554688,478.4599914550781,530.406005859375]},"2ceac827ba442189b8e3":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"a group of words that has a subject and a predicate but it does not","bbox":[108.05000305175781,532.6160278320312,523.6200561523438,546.6200561523438]},"a31959dfe7ab3e6ca9e9":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"expresses a complete thought and it cannot stand alone","bbox":[144.0500030517578,548.8159790039062,476.6099853515625,562.8200073242188]}},"excerptBlocks":{"concepts:0":["bcdac6a925d3bcce2a48","796a551743c2d9377d6d","332f72e9b45a43960eb8","094b8511819977e48212","d66b889549a4f31ff8bf","3e1f0b0ed2d79e99e10f","bc271189183d0bb23968","fccac3bdf8ec39ebb881","2ceac827ba442189b8e3","a31959dfe7ab3e6ca9e9"],"concepts:1":["9efcfbfa8b2c743b9002","23c78202d57473e0c69c","9e03646fcf7c473307d1","b355d60b191cd33788d8","6a94a674baa243cbb4a8","85e0e1ba6bcc3b5d4aa4","0ecbe122eaab21979c58","4aba4eaa0f0f7f028adc","c24fb97f86aaac45ae47","1cde296